{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And we shall live and not die.</b> In every place where such a doubling is said, it is expounded regarding the World to Come, as Rashi explained in parashat VeZot HaBerakhah (Deuteronomy 33:6): ״יְחִי רְאוּבֵן בָּעוֹלָם הַזֶּה וְאַל יָמוּת לָעוֹלָם הַבָּא״ — 'Let Reuben live in this world and let him not die in the World to Come,' and as was explained above in parashat Noaḥ (Genesis 6:15) on the verse ״לֹא אָמוּת כִּי אֶחְיֶה״ — 'I shall not die, for I shall live.' Therefore this doubling requires explanation here as well. And what seems right to me to say concerning this is that he said it both toward Jacob his father and toward himself, for Jacob had been informed that if one of his sons does not die in his lifetime, he will not see the face of Gehenna (see Tanḥuma Vayigash 9), and through the mission of Benjamin it would be revealed that Joseph is alive, and then Jacob would live in this world, as it is written (Genesis 45:27) ״וַתְּחִי רוּחַ יַעֲקֹב אֲבִיהֶם״ — 'and the spirit of Jacob their father revived,' and he would not die also in the World to Come, as it is written (Genesis 46:30) ״אָמוּתָה הַפַּעַם״ — 'I shall die this time' — once and not twice. And the spirit of YHWH spoke through Judah what he said: ״וְנִחְיֶה״ — we shall live — in this world, ״וְלֹא נָמוּת״ — and not die — in the World to Come, also you, for the reason that was explained. However, ״גַּם אֲנַחְנוּ״ — also we — the intent was regarding himself, for he accepted upon himself ״אִם לֹא הֲבִיאֹתִיו אֵלֶיךָ וְחָטָאתִי לְאָבִי כָּל הַיָּמִים״ (Genesis 43:9) — that is, for the World to Come. Therefore he said that he would be certain to bring him, and this is what he said: ״שִׁלְחָה הַנַּעַר אִתִּי וְנָקוּמָה וְנֵלֵכָה וְנִחְיֶה״ — send the lad with me and we will arise and go and live — in this world, in the ravages of famine, for the year of famine will not harm us; ״וְלֹא נָמוּת״ — and not die — in the World to Come, because of the ban he accepted upon himself, for he said with certainty: I shall bring him to you. And what he said, ״אֲנַחְנוּ״ — we — included all the brothers with him, for if Joseph had died and Jacob had seen the face of Gehenna, then all the brothers who brought Jacob to such a measure would not be assured of escaping the sight of the face of Gehenna, for the one who causes a stumbling block — it is justice that he too should stumble through it." "And what he said, ״גַּם טַפֵּנוּ״ — also our little ones — is because it is said (Proverbs 11:26) ״מֹנֵעַ בָּר יִקְּבֻהוּ לְאוֹם״ — 'he who withholds grain, the people will curse him.' And our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded (Sanhedrin 91a) that even fetuses in their mother's womb curse him, for לְאוֹם means nothing but fetuses, as it is said (Genesis 25:23) ״וּלְאֹם מִלְאֹם יֶאֱמָץ״. Even though they expounded there (Sanhedrin 91a) ״מֹנֵעַ בָּר״ as referring to one who withholds halakhah from a student, nevertheless the verse does not depart from its plain sense and speaks of grain in the literal sense, for the one who withholds produce — then the women are hungry and this harms also the fetuses in their wombs, and all the more so their little children, for they will say to their mothers ״אַיֵּה דָּגָן וְתִירוֹשׁ״ — 'where is grain and wine?' Therefore Judah said ״גַּם טַפֵּנוּ״ — also our little ones — they shall live in this world, and not die in the World to Come, for if through you grain is withheld from them, then they will curse you and they will be children of death in the World to Come according to the law of one who curses his father, for children's children are regarded as children (Yevamot 62b). And even though they are small and not subject to punishment, nevertheless the sin requires some scouring to remove the blemish of the soul and its rust. And according to its plain sense he said ״גַּם אֲנַחְנוּ״ first, for the life of a man takes precedence over the life of his father, and the life of his father takes precedence over the life of the little ones. Therefore he arranged it in this order: ״גַּם אֲנַחְנוּ גַּם אַתָּה גַּם טַפֵּנוּ״ — also we, also you, also our little ones."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 43, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:33:30.132480073Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}